; FILED AGAINST THE VM: `(tensor-shape #())` — native is the correct side here.
;
; `#()` is a rank-1 tensor of extent 0 (lib/frontend/parser.cpp parse_vector_body:
; "Empty vector: #() -> 1D tensor with 0 elements"), so its shape is `(0)`: one
; dimension, whose length is zero. Native answers that. The VM answers the EMPTY
; list on master, and an empty VECTOR on the branch that reworked it — neither of
; which is a shape this language produces anywhere else.
;
;   (tensor-shape #(1.0 2.0))              native (2)   vm (2)      ; agree
;   (tensor-shape #())                     native (0)   vm ()       ; diverge
;   (pair? (tensor-shape #()))             native #t    vm #f
;   (vector? (tensor-shape #()))           native #f    vm #f
;   (length (tensor-shape #()))            native 1     vm 0
;   (tensor-shape (make-tensor (list 0) 1.0))
;                                          native (0)   vm  <no tensor-length/make path>
;
; Three independent reasons native is right:
;
;   1. RETURN TYPE. `tensor-shape` returns a LIST of dimensions — `(3)`, `(2 3)`,
;      `(2 2 2)` — and the VM produces a list for every non-empty tensor too. Its
;      answer for the empty tensor alone is not a one-element list, so
;      `(car (tensor-shape t))` and `(length (tensor-shape t))` work for every
;      tensor except one. `(length (tensor-shape t))` is the tensor's rank
;      natively for all t; on the VM it is the rank for all t except `#()`, where
;      it silently answers 0.
;
;   2. RANK IS INFORMATION. `()` as a shape means rank 0 — a scalar. `#()` is not
;      a scalar; it has one dimension whose extent happens to be zero. Collapsing
;      "rank 1, extent 0" to "rank 0" discards the rank, and there is then no way
;      to tell `#()` from a genuine 0-dimensional tensor.
;
;   3. PRIOR ART. numpy, the model this operator follows, gives
;      `np.array([]).shape == (0,)` — a one-element tuple, not an empty one.
;      docs/API_REFERENCE.md documents shapes as lists throughout.
;
; So no native change was made for this case. The VM should return the
; one-element list `(0)`; then this file can move into corpus/.

(display (tensor-shape #(1.0 2.0)))
(newline)
(display (tensor-shape #()))
(newline)
(display (list (pair? (tensor-shape #())) (vector? (tensor-shape #()))))
(newline)
(display (length (tensor-shape #())))
(newline)
